<div class="meta">Lesson 0026 · LLM systems · from PR #134 (#125–#129, milestone #16)</div>
<h1>Generate, Validate, Retry: the Validator IS the Feature</h1>
<p>"Describe a village, get a playable map" shipped tonight — and the model
never places a single tile. It fills a schema; a validator gates the ONE
load path; invalid output goes back to the model with the validator's own
error messages; and after three failures the feature reports plainly
instead of loading garbage. The generation prompt took an evening of
iteration. The validator is what made that iteration <em>possible</em>.</p>

<h2>The loop, concretely (worldgen_cli / main.cpp)</h2>
<pre><code>for (attempt = 1..3):
    raw  = model(systemPrompt, description + feedback)
    json = extractJsonObject(raw)          // fences/prose tolerated
    map, cast = parse(json)                // shape only
    errors = validateMap(map) + validateCast(cast) + validateVillageLinks(...)
    if errors.empty(): load()              // the ONLY entrance
    feedback = renderRetryFeedback(errors) // the errors, verbatim</code></pre>
<p>Two design rules give the loop its power:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Errors are written for the model.</strong> Not "invalid part" but
<code>unknown part: hair_dreads (nearest valid id: hair_braids)</code>; not
"bad trait" but the full list of valid ids inline. The validator's error
strings ARE the retry prompt — one artifact, two audiences.</li>
<li><strong>No auto-repair.</strong> The tempting fix — silently substitute
the nearest id, nudge the overlapping building — would hide model quality,
poison the benchmark, and ship surprises. Wrapper normalization is the one
exception (stripping the brackets from a trait emitted as the string
<code>"[grumpy]"</code>): the content is untouched, only JSON-ish packaging.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The night's real lesson: indicting the contract</h2>
<p>First live runs: <strong>all three</strong> local models failed with the
same shape error, three attempts each. When one model fails, suspect the
model. When every model fails <em>identically</em>, suspect YOUR contract.
Ours asked for persona files as newline-embedded JSON strings — genuinely
awkward to emit. Switching to one JSON <em>object</em> per character (and
converting to persona text on our side, so the one-loader rule held)
cleared the shape failures instantly.</p>
<p>Second round: <code>body_round and eyes_angry mix incompatible
styles</code> — every model, repeatedly. The prompt demanded
"style-consistent" looks but the vocabulary never said which family any
part belonged to. The instruction was <em>unsatisfiable</em>. Annotating
each id — <code>body_round(round), eyes_angry(blocky)</code> — took
qwen3:8b from 0-for-3-attempts to <strong>valid on the first try</strong>.</p>

<div class="callout good"><strong>Diagnostic ladder for generation
failures:</strong> identical failures across models → fix the contract;
consistent category of failure on one model → fix the prompt (usually
missing information, not missing emphasis); scattered failures that survive
both → measure models and pick by validity rate, never by label.</div>

<h2>Closed world, measured models</h2>
<p>The catalogs (pieces, parts, palettes, traits) are the model's entire
vocabulary — generation can never introduce content, only compose what the
game already renders. And the model choice is a <em>measurement</em>:
<code>bench_schema_models.py</code> drives the same CLI over a fixed corpus
and reports validity / first-attempt / latency per model. The bench calls
the C++ validator through the CLI rather than re-implementing rules in
Python — one contract, everywhere.</p>

<h2>Two load strictnesses, on purpose</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Path</th><th>Policy</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hand-made map opened in the editor</td><td>Repairable: bad entries drop with a toast, the map opens</td><td>A human can fix their own file; locking them out helps nobody</td></tr>
<tr><td>LLM output</td><td>Strict: any error → retry → plain failure; nothing partial ever loads</td><td>The model gets feedback instead of forgiveness; quality stays measurable</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout warn"><strong>The tell.</strong> If a generative
feature's output can reach the game without passing the validator — a debug
path, a "trusted" mode, a load-then-check — the validator is decoration,
not architecture. Grep for the second entrance; it always appears
eventually unless the design forbids it.</div>
